
The sync event stream lets an external application find out what the SymmetricDS engine is doing without reading SymmetricDS tables. The
engine records an event whenever it starts or stops, whenever a batch finishes, and whenever a load starts or finishes. A client subscribes to
these events through the REST API and receives them as a stream of Server-Sent Events, which is the `text/event-stream` format defined by the
HTML specification. Most languages have an `EventSource` client or a library that parses this format.

The sync event stream requires an enterprise license.

=== Enabling the Stream

Events are only recorded when the `sync.event.enabled` parameter is `true`, which is not the default. The REST API must also be enabled with the
`rest.api.enable` parameter, and the client must send a REST API key (see <<Rest Keys>>).

[cols="3,^1,5"]
|===
|Parameter|Default|Description

|sync.event.enabled |false |Records sync events so they can be delivered to the stream.
|rest.stream.keepalive.seconds |5 |Seconds without an event before the stream sends a keepalive.
|rest.stream.buffer.events.max |500 |Maximum number of events held in memory for a connected client. When the buffer is full, the events are still delivered from the database.
|rest.stream.poll.seconds |1 |Seconds between checks of the database for events recorded by other members of a cluster. Only used when clustering is enabled.
|purge.sync.event.retention.minutes |10080 |Minutes to keep recorded events before the purge job deletes them (7 days).
|===

If the REST API is disabled, the endpoint is not available. If `sync.event.enabled` is `false` or the engine does not have an enterprise
license, the request is rejected with HTTP status 403.

=== Connecting

[source]
----
GET /api/engine/{engine}/syncevents/stream
----

.Request headers
[cols="2,5"]
|===
|Header|Description

|X-REST-API-KEY |The REST API key. A missing or rejected key results in HTTP status 659.
|Accept |Should be `text/event-stream`. When it is missing or does not allow `text/event-stream`, a warning is logged and the stream is sent anyway.
|Last-Event-ID |Optional. The `id` of the last event the client received. See <<Resuming After a Disconnect>>.
|===

.Example
----
curl -N -H "X-REST-API-KEY: <key>" -H "Accept: text/event-stream" \
  "http://localhost:31415/api/engine/server/syncevents/stream"
----

The response stays open until the client disconnects or the engine stops. Only one client at a time is supported. Delivery is tracked once per event,
not once per client, so when more than one client is connected to the same engine, a client can miss events that another client already received,
and a warning is logged.

.Response status codes
[cols="1,5"]
|===
|Status|Meaning

|200 |The stream was opened.
|400 |The `Last-Event-ID` header is not the UUID of an event.
|403 |Sync events are not enabled, or the engine is not licensed for them.
|659 |The REST API key is missing or not accepted.
|===

=== Stream Format

Each message in the stream is separated by a blank line. An event has an `id:` line, an `event:` line that holds the event type, and a `data:`
line that holds a single line of JSON.

.Example batch event
----
id: 6f1c2d3e-4a5b-4c6d-8e7f-0a1b2c3d4e5f
event: batch
data: {"eventId":"6f1c2d3e-4a5b-4c6d-8e7f-0a1b2c3d4e5f","type":"batch","version":"2026-10-01","direction":"incoming","batchId":1042,"nodeId":"store-001","channelId":"item","tables":["ITEM","ITEM_SELLING_RETAIL"],"status":"OK","loadId":0,"byteCount":2150,"rowsLoaded":12,"insertRows":10,"updateRows":2,"deleteRows":0}

----

The JSON data of every event holds these fields, along with the fields of its event type.

[cols="2,5"]
|===
|Field|Description

|eventId |A unique identifier for the event, which is also sent as the `id:` line. Not present on `status` events.
|type |The event type, which is also sent as the `event:` line.
|version |The version of the event format, currently `2026-10-01`. New fields may be added to an event type without changing the version.
|===

Events are delivered in the order they were recorded. Delivery is at least once, so a client should ignore an `eventId` that it has already processed.

=== Event Types

[cols="1,5"]
|===
|Type|Sent when

|status |The first message after connecting. It reports the engine name and whether the engine is starting, started, stopped, or failed.
|engine |The engine starts or stops.
|batch |A batch reaches status OK or ER on either side of the synchronization.
|load |A load starts, completes, or is cancelled on either side of the synchronization.
|keepalive |No event has been sent for `rest.stream.keepalive.seconds`.
|===

==== Status

The `status` event is sent first on every new connection, including one that resumes with `Last-Event-ID`, so the client knows which engine it is
connected to and whether the engine is ready. It has no `id:` line, is not recorded, and is not replayed.

.Example
----
event: status
data: {"type":"status","version":"2026-10-01","engineName":"server","status":"started"}

----

[cols="2,5"]
|===
|Field|Description

|engineName |The name of the engine.
|status |One of `starting`, `started`, `stopped`, or `failed`.
|===

==== Engine

[cols="2,5"]
|===
|Field|Description

|status |One of `started`, `stopped`, or `failed`.
|engineName |The name of the engine.
|nodeId |The node ID of the engine, if it has one yet.
|error |The reason the engine failed. Only present when the status is `failed`.
|===

==== Batch

A batch event is sent when a batch finishes on the node that created it (outgoing) and on the node that loaded it (incoming).

[cols="2,5"]
|===
|Field|Description

|direction |`outgoing` when the node sent the batch, or `incoming` when the node received it.
|batchId |The ID of the batch.
|nodeId |The ID of the node on the other side of the synchronization.
|channelId |The channel of the batch.
|tables |The names of the tables in the batch.
|status |`OK` when the batch was successful, or `ER` when it failed.
|loadId |The ID of the load that the batch belongs to, or 0 when the batch is not part of a load.
|byteCount |The size of the batch in bytes.
|rowsExtracted |Outgoing only. The number of rows that were extracted.
|rowsLoaded |The number of rows that were loaded.
|insertRows, updateRows, deleteRows |Incoming only. The number of rows loaded by each kind of change.
|failedTable, failedRowNumber |Incoming and `ER` only, when known. The table and the row number where loading failed.
|sqlState, sqlCode, sqlMessage, failedLineNumber |`ER` only. Details of the error that stopped the batch.
|===

The `tables` field is taken from the summary of the batch (the `summary` column of <<OUTGOING_BATCH>> and <<INCOMING_BATCH>>). That column holds 254 characters at most, so
for a batch with many tables with long names, the list is cut off and the last name in it may end with `...`.

==== Load

A load event is sent when a load starts and again when it completes or is cancelled. A load event is sent on the node that sends the data,
and on the node that receives it, so the stream of each node shows the loads that it takes part in. A load can span several channels and tables.

[cols="2,5"]
|===
|Field|Description

|status |`started`, `completed`, or `cancelled`.
|loadId |The ID of the load.
|sourceNodeId |The ID of the node that sends the data.
|targetNodeId |The ID of the node that receives the data.
|isFullLoad |`true` for a full load, or `false` when only some tables are reloaded.
|rowsLoaded |The number of rows loaded so far.
|tableCount |The number of tables in the load.
|tables |The names of the tables in the load.
|channels |The names of the channels that carry the load.
|===

On the node that receives a load, the lists of `tables` and `channels` are built from the extract requests that have been synchronized to it.
They can be incomplete in the `started` event if those requests have not arrived yet, and the `completed` event shows the full list.

==== Keepalive

When no event has been sent for `rest.stream.keepalive.seconds`, the stream sends a keepalive so the client and any proxy in between can tell
that the connection is still alive, and so the engine notices when the client has gone away. It is an `event: keepalive` line and a comment line.

----
event: keepalive
: keepalive

----

Because a line that starts with a colon is a comment and the message has no `data:` line, the HTML specification says clients must ignore it: an `EventSource` does not raise an
event for it and it does not change the last event ID. A client that wants to track the keepalives can read the raw stream.

=== Resuming After a Disconnect

Events are kept in the database for `purge.sync.event.retention.minutes`. When a client has to reconnect, it can send the `id` of the last event it received as
the `Last-Event-ID` header, and the stream starts with the events that were recorded after it. An `EventSource` client sends this header automatically when it reconnects.

If no event has that ID, which is usually because it was already purged, all of the retained events are sent and a warning is logged. A header that is not a UUID is rejected with HTTP status 400.

=== Limitations

* Events are recorded and delivered once per batch status change. Changes to a batch outside of normal synchronization, such as ignoring a batch or manually updating its status in the database, do not produce events.
* A client that disconnects and doesn't send `Last-Event-ID` when it connects again receives only the events that have not been delivered yet.
* Events are marked as delivered after they are written to the connection, so an event can be sent again after a failure, but is not lost.
* In a cluster, the events of all members are recorded in the same database and are delivered by whichever member the client is connected to.
